<span class="kicker">Who runs this</span>
<h1>About</h1>
<p class="lede">I have had my hands in this water twice a day for four years, and until last August I could not have told you one number about what was in it.</p>
<p>My name is Haolin Li. I am a senior at Rutherford High School and I have rowed at the Nereid Boat Club since I was fourteen. Four years of that is roughly two thousand outings of gripping an oar handle that has been in the Passaic, wiping my face with the back of my hand, and eating in the car afterwards without thinking about it. In June a novice asked me whether the water was clean and I gave her the answer everyone at the club gives, which is that you do not swallow it. Then I went looking for the number behind that answer and could not find one for this stretch.</p>
<p>There is agency data on the Lower Passaic, and quite a lot of it. The New Jersey Harbor Dischargers Group has sampled at the Dundee Dam and at Union Avenue for years and the results are public, which is exactly why the model on the methods page could be trained on something other than my own numbers. What did not exist was a weekly count for the water at our dock, published before Saturday, in a form a seventeen year old in a single can act on. That is the gap, and it is a narrow one.</p>
<p>Ten Saturdays later there is a record with no gaps in it, five people who can take a sample properly, a hundred and fifty people who get the Friday note, and a dataset anyone can download. The count that surprised me was not the 2,400 after the storm. It was 26 September, wet and clean, which is the single point that stops the rain rule from being a law.</p>
<p>I own a rain gauge and check it before I check the weather. My mother thinks this is the least useful hobby a seventeen year old has ever taken up, and she may be right, though she does ask me on Friday nights what color tomorrow is.</p>

<h2>The season, in one row</h2>
<p>Ten Saturdays, no gaps. Every square is a week you can open.</p>
