<h2>The crew</h2>
<p>Five people can now take a sample, pour a plate and count it. That is the difference between a project and a record, because a record survives one person having a regatta.</p>
<div class="crew"><div><p class="who">Haolin Li</p><p class="does">founder, samples most Saturdays, counts the plates</p></div><div><p class="who">Marisol T.</p><p class="does">sampler, sampled beside me through September while training and covered the mornings I was away racing</p></div><div><p class="who">Dev P.</p><p class="does">sampler, runs the turbidity tube and fills in the field sheet</p></div><div><p class="who">Aiden K.</p><p class="does">trained in September, covers the October Saturdays</p></div><div><p class="who">Grace L.</p><p class="does">keeps the list and sends the Friday note when I cannot</p></div></div>

<h2>What this is not</h2>
<p>I am not speaking for the boat club. I row there and the advisory goes up on its door, and that is the whole of the relationship; nothing here is a club position and no officer of it has signed off on anything. I do not speak for NJDEP, EPA, USGS or the sewerage commission either. Where this site uses their numbers it cites the document and the date it was read, and where it makes a claim about the river, that claim comes from my own plates.</p>

<h2>Next season</h2>
<ul class="bul">
<li>Start in April rather than August, which doubles the record and catches the spring rain.</li>
<li>A second station at another club's dock on this stretch, running the same protocol, so a wet Saturday can be seen in two places at once.</li>
<li>More splits, ideally ten rather than three, and taken by two different samplers.</li>
<li>Cross check the boathouse gauge against a nearby official precipitation record, which is the one loose end in the rainfall column.</li>
</ul>
